{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1945","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1945","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1945\",\"parent_revision\":\"29892e4c783c39da7980aa2477b6b62a78f7cfd7\",\"root_revision\":\"31bf9716173d6f8e9282c95e204ac27f936860abbfa3bd16efb3464ca3d70f31\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Claim 1 \u2014 monitor.py registry path uses old flat-file format\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: monitor.py:_run_monitor() uses registry-{session_id}.json but spawn.py has migrated to per-entry sessions/{session_id}/{name}.json\\nevidence: Read tool \u2014 monitor.py line 137: `return state_dir / \\\"kage-bunshin\\\" / f\\\"registry-{session_id}.json\\\"`. spawn.py lines 211-221: `_session_entries_dir` returns `state_dir / \\\"sessions\\\" / session_id`. spawn.py design comment lines 193-209 explicitly states the old format caused data loss under parallel writes and was replaced.\\nsource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py` line 137; `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py` lines 193-235\\n\\n### Claim 2 \u2014 kage-bunshin does not bind TCP ports itself\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: kage-bunshin does not bind TCP ports \u2014 scope of orphan-port detection is child processes of spawned sessions\\nevidence: Read tool \u2014 spawn.py spawns via `tmux new-session ... claude --worktree {name} --tmux --dangerously-skip-permissions --model {model}` (spawn.py lines 40-44 in SKILL.md quick start). No socket bind, no server start, no port parameter in any spawn.py function. monitor.py has zero references to `lsof`, `socket`, `port`, `bind`, or `orphan_port` (confirmed by description grep claims returning zero matches).\\nsource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py` \u2014 no port operations; item description: \\\"Grep of plugins/development-harness/ for lsof, orphan_port, port_check returns zero matches\\\"\\n\\n### Claim 3 \u2014 dispatch_state.DispatchStateManager tracks PID at wave-item level only\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: DispatchStateManager tracks PID liveness (dispatch_stale_check) but only at wave-item level; child servers spawned by tasks are not tracked\\nevidence: Item description states this explicitly; confirmed by CLAUDE.md plugin docs: \\\"dispatch_stale_check(milestone_number) \u2014 Check whether any wave items have stale or dead PIDs\\\"; no child-process enumeration in dispatch state API.\\nsource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 dispatch state MCP tools section\\n\\n### Claim 4 \u2014 monitor.py poll currently fails for all spawn.py sessions\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: monitor.py poll emits {\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"message\\\": \\\"registry not found: ...\\\"} for all sessions created by current spawn.py\\nevidence: Read tool \u2014 _run_monitor() line 334: `if not reg_path.exists(): _emit_error(f\\\"registry not found: {reg_path}\\\"); sys.exit(1)`. Since spawn.py no longer creates `registry-{session_id}.json` (uses per-entry directory), this check always fails for current sessions.\\nsource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py` lines 332-341\\n\\n### Claim 5 \u2014 lsof is available on Linux/macOS for orphan port detection\\n\\nverdict: INCONCLUSIVE\\nclaim: lsof -i -n -P can enumerate child processes listening on ports\\nevidence: lsof is standard on Linux and macOS but is not guaranteed in all CI or container environments. shutil.which(\\\"lsof\\\") pattern used elsewhere in monitor.py for tmux presence check validates this approach is viable. No direct lsof execution tested in this environment.\\nsource: Pattern reference from monitor.py lines 192-199 (shutil.which(\\\"tmux\\\") pattern); standard Unix tool documentation\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: feat: Add orphan-port detection and cleanup to kage-bunshin monitor\\nDate: 2026-05-02\\nGoal: Add orphan-process/port detection and cleanup to kage-bunshin monitor so that child processes left running after a session ends can be identified and killed, and the registry path mismatch in monitor.py is resolved.\\nConditions:\\n1. monitor.py registry path bug | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Read tool \u2014 monitor.py line 137 returns registry-{session_id}.json; spawn.py lines 193-235 confirm per-entry sessions/{session_id}/{name}.json is canonical\\n2. spawn.py registry format | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Read tool \u2014 spawn.py _session_entries_dir() lines 211-221; _load_registry() lines 238-264\\n3. lsof availability | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: [AUTO] Resolved: lsof is standard on Linux/macOS; shutil.which(\\\"lsof\\\") pattern already used in monitor.py for tmux guard (lines 192-199); runtime guard approach validated\\n4. PID\u2192session mapping | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: spawn.py registry entries store tmux_session field; session name cross-reference with lsof PID output is derivable via /proc or pgrep\\n5. Definition of orphan port scope | Snapshot: MISSING \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: [AUTO] Resolved: scope = TCP port detection via lsof -i -n -P on child processes whose parent kage-bunshin session has terminated \u2014 sourced from item Target State section; kage-bunshin itself confirmed to not bind ports (fact-check claim 2: VERIFIED)\\n6. Cleanup trigger condition | Snapshot: MISSING \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: [AUTO] Resolved: orphan defined as: session present in registry with terminated tmux session + child PID still holding a port \u2014 sourced from item Target State: \\\"registry says done but child PID still listening\\\"; trigger = session status done + child port listener alive\\n7. Registry path fix scope | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-check claim 4 VERIFIED: _run_monitor() lines 332-341 always fails for current sessions; fix is prerequisite for functional monitor.py poll\\n8. DispatchStateManager interaction | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: CLAUDE.md dispatch state docs confirm wave-item-level PID tracking only; no child subprocess tracking; no change required to dispatch layer\\n\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 5: MISSING \u2192 AVAILABLE [AUTO] Resolved \u2014 scope derived from Target State section, kage-bunshin port-bind confirmed absent\\n- Condition 6: MISSING \u2192 AVAILABLE [AUTO] Resolved \u2014 trigger condition derived from Target State description\\n- Condition 3: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE \u2014 lsof runtime guard pattern verified from monitor.py tmux pattern\\n- Condition 7: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE \u2014 fact-checker VERIFIED registry path bug always fails\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **add orphan-port detection and cleanup to kage-bunshin monitor** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/developer-tools/abtop.md \u2014 pattern: OrphanPort detection via lsof scanning, with X-key kill-all action\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-25\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-26)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n**Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS**\\n\\n| Input | Condition | Notes |\\n|-------|-----------|-------|\\n| Functional intent | AVAILABLE | kage-bunshin does not bind TCP ports; scope is orphaned tmux sessions, stale registry entries, abandoned git worktrees |\\n| spawn.py registry format | AVAILABLE | per-entry sessions/{session_id}/{name}.json confirmed at spawn.py lines 194-218 |\\n| monitor.py current implementation | AVAILABLE | monitor.py lines 127-383 read in full; registry path bug at line 137 |\\n| Definition of orphan port | PARTIAL | kage-bunshin does not bind TCP ports \u2014 needs human confirmation of scope |\\n| Whether monitor registry path bug is in-scope | PARTIAL | monitor.py uses stale registry-{session_id}.json while spawn.py uses sessions/{session_id}/{name}.json |\\n| Cleanup trigger conditions | MISSING | No specification of when an orphan is declared vs a slow-starting session |\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\nSteps to reproduce the registry path mismatch failure (confirmed defect):\\n\\n1. Ensure a kage-bunshin registry exists from a recent spawn.py run: `ls ~/.dh/projects/-home-user-claude_skills/kage-bunshin/sessions/`\\n2. Note the session_id used (e.g. `test`)\\n3. Run monitor poll: `uv run plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py poll --session-id test`\\n4. Observe output: `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"message\\\": \\\"registry not found: ~/.dh/projects/.../kage-bunshin/registry-test.json\\\"}`\\n5. Verify the per-entry directory exists: `ls ~/.dh/projects/-home-user-claude_skills/kage-bunshin/sessions/test/`\\n6. Observe: session JSON files exist in the per-entry directory but monitor.py looked for the old flat-file format\\n\\nFor orphan port symptom (motivating scenario):\\n\\n1. Spawn a kage-bunshin session whose task starts a background server (e.g. `python -m http.server 8765 &`)\\n2. Let the session complete and the tmux session die\\n3. Check: `lsof -i :8765` \u2014 port still in use by orphaned python process\\n4. Start a new wave that also tries port 8765 \u2014 wave fails with address-in-use error\\n5. No audit trail shows which prior session left port 8765 open\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 monitor.py poll is completely non-functional for all current spawn.py sessions due to registry path mismatch. Orphan port detection prevents cascading wave failures.\\n\\nEffort: Medium \u2014 registry path fix is Small; orphan port detection is Medium.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Any kage-bunshin fleet orchestration that calls `monitor.py poll` \u2014 currently returns error for 100% of spawned sessions\\n- Bottleneck: Wave-level retry loops that hit port conflicts from orphaned subprocesses cannot detect or clean up the conflict source; manual intervention required\\n- Affected workflows: `/dh:work-milestone` dispatch loop uses monitor.py poll for session health checks; broken registry path makes the entire monitoring layer inoperative\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- monitor.py poll becomes functional again for all current spawn.py sessions (registry path fix)\\n- Port-conflict wave failures become self-healing: orphan detection identifies the blocking process and kill-orphans cleans it up before retry\\n- Audit trail: `orphan_ports` JSON output records which session left which port open, enabling post-mortem analysis\\n- Session resource accounting becomes accurate: RAM limit computation uses live sessions only (no stale registry inflation)\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nAfter this item is implemented:\\n\\n- `monitor.py poll --session-id {id}` reads the per-entry registry directory `sessions/{id}/` correctly and returns `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"all_complete\\\"}` or `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"intervention_needed\\\"}` or `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"timeout\\\"}` \u2014 never `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"message\\\": \\\"registry not found\\\"}`\\n- `monitor.py audit-ports` runs lsof cross-referenced against the session registry and emits JSON listing any ports held by processes whose parent kage-bunshin session has terminated\\n- `monitor.py kill-orphans` sends SIGTERM then SIGKILL (after 5s) to orphaned PIDs; afterwards `lsof -i :{port}` returns no results for those ports\\n- When a kage-bunshin session terminates, its child subprocesses that remain alive and listening on ports are detectable and cleanable without manual intervention\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `uv run plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py poll --session-id {id}` after spawning at least one session exits with `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"all_complete\\\"}` or `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"intervention_needed\\\"}` \u2014 never `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"message\\\": \\\"registry not found\\\"}`\\n2. `monitor.py poll` reads the per-entry registry at `sessions/{session_id}/` matching spawn.py's format \u2014 no code path reaches the old `registry-{session_id}.json`\\n3. After a tmux session is force-killed (`tmux kill-session -t {name}`), `monitor.py poll` detects the session as dead and eventually emits `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"all_complete\\\"}`\\n4. `monitor.py audit-ports` (or named equivalent) emits JSON with a field listing ports held by orphaned processes; output includes `port`, `pid`, and `owning_session_id` per entry\\n5. After running `monitor.py kill-orphans`, `lsof -i :{port}` for each previously orphaned port returns no results\\n6. If `shutil.which('lsof')` returns None, orphan-detection subcommands emit a structured error rather than crashing\\n7. All new subcommands exit with code 0 on success and code 1 on fatal error, outputting JSON status objects consistent with existing monitor.py output format\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- Does orphan-port detection refer to TCP port detection or to orphaned tmux sessions, git worktrees, and stale registry entries?\\n- Should the monitor.py registry path mismatch (currently always errors for current spawn.py sessions) be fixed in this item or tracked separately?\\n- When is a session considered orphaned vs still legitimately running? Is there a timeout threshold?\\n- Should cleanup be a spawn.py prune subcommand, spawn.py list --prune flag, or automatic on spawn/list?\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Skill | /dh:kage-bunshin |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py |\\n| File | plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/health.py |\\n| Prior work | Research entry: ./research/developer-tools/abtop.md \u2014 OrphanPort detection via lsof scanning pattern |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n| File | Role |\\n|------|------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py` | Primary change target \u2014 fix `_registry_path()`, add `audit-ports` and `kill-orphans` subcommands |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py` | Reference for canonical per-entry path derivation (`_session_entries_dir`, lines 211-221) |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/SKILL.md` | Documentation \u2014 update monitor.py usage section |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/health.py` | Unaffected \u2014 health subcommand uses JSONL/teams data not registry |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 registry path fix is self-contained; orphan detection depends only on lsof availability at runtime\\n- Blocks: Any milestone dispatch workflow calling monitor.py poll (currently broken for all sessions); related: #1069 (orchestrator loses team state across context compaction) benefits from improved monitoring reliability\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- orphan-port definition not confirmed \u2014 scope of cleanup target is ambiguous\\n- Cleanup trigger conditions not specified\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 Registry path fix is Small (one function, `_registry_path()` at monitor.py line 137, clear root cause, straightforward update to match spawn.py's `_session_entries_dir` pattern at lines 211-221). Orphan port detection is Medium (new CLI subcommands, lsof subprocess integration, PID\u2192session registry cross-reference, SIGTERM/SIGKILL escalation with 5s timeout, JSON output format, shutil.which guard for lsof absence). Total effort: Medium.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/SKILL.md` \u2014 instructs AI on monitor.py usage patterns; must reflect any new subcommands\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py::_load_registry` \u2014 aggregates `sessions/{session_id}/*.json`; compatible with any monitor.py fix that reads the same directory structure (spawn.py lines 238-264)\\n- Any orchestrator script calling `monitor.py poll --session-id` \u2014 currently always receives `{\\\"status\\\": \\\"error\\\", \\\"message\\\": \\\"registry not found: ...\\\"}` due to path mismatch; will receive correct status after fix\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/dispatch_state.py::DispatchStateManager::dispatch_stale_check` \u2014 tracks PID liveness at wave-item level only; does not track child subprocesses spawned by tasks; no code change required unless orphan tracking is extended to dispatch state\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/health.py` \u2014 imports no registry-path logic; health subcommand uses `~/.claude/teams/` and JSONL session files; unaffected by registry path change\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py::_session_entries_dir` \u2014 defines the canonical per-entry directory path `sessions/{session_id}/`; monitor.py fix must use the same derivation (spawn.py lines 211-221)\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py::_registry_path` \u2014 produces the registry file path; currently returns `kage-bunshin/registry-{session_id}.json` (flat-file); must be updated to match spawn.py's per-entry `sessions/{session_id}/` directory format (confirmed line 137)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py::_run_monitor` \u2014 consumes the registry path from `_registry_path()`; fails with \\\"registry not found\\\" for all current spawn.py sessions because the path format diverged\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py` (new `audit-ports` / `kill-orphans` subcommands) \u2014 would produce lsof-based orphan port detection output if in-scope\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\nAlignment assessment: DIVERGENT\\n\\n**Comparison**: The item title \\\"Add orphan-port detection and cleanup\\\" aligns with the \\\"Target State\\\" section which describes `lsof -i -n -P` scanning and `monitor.py kill-orphans`. However the existing \\\"Groomed (2026-04-26)\\\" Expected Behavior and Acceptance Criteria sections describe a different scope: fixing the registry path mismatch so `monitor.py poll` works correctly. The two scopes are:\\n\\n1. **Registry path fix** (Groomed 2026-04-26 scope): Align monitor.py's `_registry_path()` with spawn.py's per-entry `sessions/{session_id}/` format. ACs describe poll exit statuses, dead session detection, and worktree cleanup.\\n\\n2. **Orphan port detection** (Target State scope): New `audit-ports` subcommand using `lsof -i -n -P`, PID\u2192session cross-reference, `kill-orphans` subcommand with SIGTERM/SIGKILL escalation.\\n\\n**Divergence**: The existing Acceptance Criteria (from 2026-04-26 grooming) describe scope (1) \u2014 registry path fix and session lifecycle cleanup \u2014 but the item title and Target State describe scope (2) \u2014 TCP orphan port detection. These are complementary but distinct deliverables with different implementation surfaces.\\n\\n**Recommendation for planning**: Treat both as in-scope since (1) is a prerequisite for (2) \u2014 monitor.py poll must work before orphan detection can be layered on. The registry path fix is a contained defect (Small effort). The orphan port detection is the feature addition (Medium effort).\\n\\n**Current implementation vs intent**: monitor.py currently implements none of (1) or (2). spawn.py correctly implements the per-entry registry format but monitor.py has not been updated to match.\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/SKILL.md` \u2014 documents `monitor.py poll --session-id` usage; will need update if subcommands are added or registry path behavior changes\\n- Item \\\"Target State\\\" section describes `lsof -i -n -P` slow-tick behavior and `monitor.py kill-orphans` subcommand \u2014 documents intended design but is not implementation documentation\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated to new interface\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every agent instruction updated\\n- [ ] Old interface deprecated or removed (if replacing)\\n- [ ] CI/config files updated and validated\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: recurring-pattern\\n**Rationale**: This item bundles two distinct problems. (1) The registry path mismatch (monitor.py uses old flat-file format, spawn.py uses per-entry directory) is a defect \u2014 a traceable failure with identifiable cause chain: spawn.py was migrated to per-entry format without updating monitor.py. (2) The orphan-port/subprocess detection is an unbounded-design feature \u2014 no identifiable failure cause, scope is partially specified. The item appears in the backlog as a feature addition motivated by observed port-conflict symptoms on retries, which is a recurring-pattern symptom (port conflicts on wave retry when test servers aren't cleaned up). Classification: **recurring-pattern** for the port-conflict symptom; the registry path fix is a contained **defect** embedded within this item.\\n**Analysis Method**: Classification flowchart \u2014 same port-conflict problem class can appear across multiple wave executions (retry \u2192 port in use \u2192 failure)\\n**Scenario Target**: kage-bunshin fleet wave retry \u2192 port conflict on retry due to orphaned subprocess \u2192 wave failure\\n\\n### Pre-existing Bug Found\\n\\nmonitor.py:_run_monitor() at line 137 uses registry-{session_id}.json (old flat-file format) but spawn.py has been fully migrated to per-entry sessions/{session_id}/{name}.json. This means monitor.py poll is currently broken for all sessions \u2014 the monitor never successfully reads the registry created by the current spawn.py.\\n\\n### Story\\n\\nAs an orchestrator running a kage-bunshin fleet, I need the monitor to detect and clean up orphaned sessions \u2014 dead registry entries, stale tmux sessions without a corresponding registry entry, and abandoned git worktrees \u2014 so that system resources are not silently leaked and the session count remains accurate.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n| System | Role | Connection |\\n|--------|------|------------|\\n| `monitor.py` | Poll loop for tmux session state | Reads registry to get session list; compares against live tmux sessions |\\n| `spawn.py` | Session lifecycle manager | Writes per-entry JSON files to `sessions/{session_id}/{name}.json` |\\n| `health.py` | Team health inspector | Reads JSONL files and tmux panes; no registry dependency |\\n| `dispatch_state.DispatchStateManager` | Wave-item PID tracking | Tracks wave-level PIDs only; child processes out of scope |\\n| kage-bunshin registry (`sessions/{session_id}/`) | Session state storage | Per-entry JSON files; monitor.py currently reads wrong path |\\n| lsof (system tool) | Port/process enumeration | Optional new dependency for orphan-port scope |\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py` (472 lines) detects interactive state of tmux sessions (`_detect_interactive_state`, `_is_idle`, `_capture_pane`) and reads a registry file at `_registry_path()` (lines 127-145). It does not enumerate child processes of spawned `claude -p` sessions, does not run `lsof -i`, and does not detect ports left open by completed sessions. Grep of `plugins/development-harness/` for `lsof`, `orphan_port`, `port_check` returns zero matches. The dispatch state DB (`dispatch_state.DispatchStateManager`) tracks PID liveness (`dispatch_stale_check`) but only at the wave-item level \u2014 child servers spawned by a task (test servers, dev servers, MCP servers) are not tracked.\\n\\nSymptom: a kage-bunshin task that spins up `pytest --pdb`, `npm run dev`, or a test FastMCP server may leave that subprocess listening on a port after the parent `claude -p` exits (process orphaning, signal mishandling). The next wave hits a port-conflict on retry. There is no audit trail of which task left which port open.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`monitor.py` (or a new `port_audit.py` companion) runs `lsof -i -n -P` on slow ticks (every 30s, configurable) and cross-references PID \u2192 kage-bunshin session via the registry file. Output JSON includes `orphan_ports: [{port, pid, command, owning_session_id, exit_status}]` for each port held by a process whose parent kage-bunshin session has terminated (registry says `done` but child PID still listening). New `monitor.py kill-orphans` subcommand sends SIGTERM (then SIGKILL after 5s) to the orphaned PIDs. Optional integration into `dispatch_wave_status` to surface orphan counts at wave end.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nSpawn a kage-bunshin session that runs `python -m http.server 8765 &` and exits. Run `uv run plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/monitor.py audit-ports`. Output JSON contains an entry with `port: 8765` and the spawned session ID. Run `monitor.py kill-orphans` then `lsof -i :8765` returns no results. Grep of `plugins/development-harness/` for `lsof` returns at least one match in `monitor.py` or its companion.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"21053207dec483d2f2563e77b2d4700422807f669374fd84d9706dc605be1a83\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPuhLdQ\"}"}